// ============================================================================
// RESOLUTION · RUNTIME-OBSERVED EDGES
//
// A trace of the subject's own test suite, fed back into graph construction.
//
// THE ONE RULE THAT GOVERNS THIS WHOLE FILE:
//
//     A TRACE MAY ADD AN EDGE. IT MAY NEVER REMOVE ONE.
//
// A workload is a LOWER BOUND on behaviour. Everything in the trace happened, so
// every observed edge is real and may be asserted. Nothing that is ABSENT from the
// trace is thereby shown not to happen: FluentValidation's suite reaches 554 of its
// 604 methods, and the other 50 are untested, not unreachable. An engine that
// narrowed a dispatch fan to "what the tests took" would produce a graph that is
// wrong for every code path the tests do not cover, and would look BETTER by every
// precision metric while doing it. That is the trap this file is written to avoid,
// and it is why there is no rule below that deletes, filters or narrows anything.
//
// WHAT THE RUNTIME SIGNAL IS ACTUALLY FOR, then, is two things a static reading
// cannot do:
//
//   1. CLOSE A BLIND SPOT WITH A WITNESS. A site the engine resolved to nothing,
//      whose caller was observed calling something, yields an edge that is known to
//      be real. Reflection, a DI container, a source generator and `dynamic` all
//      produce edges no reading of the source can find, and this is the only
//      instrument that finds them.
//
//   2. MARK A FAN AS OBSERVED-NARROW. Where the engine named several targets and
//      the run only ever took one, the site stays multi_inferred -- the set is
//      still the sound answer -- and gains a separate fact saying which member was
//      seen. A consumer ranking call chains can use that; the graph does not
//      change shape.
//
// THE INPUT. runtime_observed_edge(CallerKey, CalleeKey, Count) is staged as facts
// by the harness, keyed on `Type.Name/paramCount` because that is the one spelling
// the instrumenter can produce without a semantic model. Empty is the normal case:
// a run with no trace behaves exactly as it did before, which is what makes this
// safe to leave switched on.
//
// A CAVEAT THE JOIN ALSO CARRIES: the caller comes off a per-thread shadow stack,
// which is exact for ordinary control flow and NOT exact across an `await`
// continuation resumed on another thread. So an observed edge is evidence about the
// CALLEE always, and about the CALLER only for synchronous frames. Rules that use
// the caller are marked.
// ============================================================================

// ── THE ENGINE'S OWN KEY FOR A METHOD ───────────────────────────────────────
// Built to match the instrumenter's, which assembles it syntactically from the
// namespace and type nest because it runs over a mirror with nothing resolved. So
// the join is on the qualified name and the parameter count, with generic argument
// lists absent from both sides.
method_runtime_key(prov, m, k) :-
    method_decl(prov, _, qn, _, m),
    method_paramc(prov, m, pc),
    k = cat(qn, cat("/", pc)).

// ── OBSERVED, AND MATCHED TO A METHOD ───────────────────────────────────────
runtime_edge_method(callerM, calleeM, n) :-
    runtime_observed_edge(ck, dk, ns),
    method_runtime_key("client", callerM, ck),
    method_runtime_key("client", calleeM, dk),
    n = to_number(ns).

// An entry the trace saw called from OUTSIDE the instrumented subtree: a test
// method, the runtime, a framework callback. The caller is not a method of the
// subject, so this is an ENTRY-POINT observation rather than an edge -- and it is
// the honest source for "what does something outside actually call", which no
// static reading of a library can answer.
runtime_entry_point(m) :-
    runtime_observed_edge("<external>", dk, _),
    method_runtime_key("client", m, dk).

// ── (1) A BLIND SPOT CLOSED BY A WITNESS ────────────────────────────────────
// The caller contains a site the engine could not resolve, and the run shows that
// caller reaching a method the engine never named from it. The edge is real: it
// happened.
//
// ATTRIBUTED TO THE CALLER, NOT TO A SITE. The tracer probes method entry, not call
// sites, so it cannot say WHICH unresolved site in the caller produced the call --
// only that the caller called it. Claiming a site would be inventing precision the
// instrument does not have. The edge is therefore emitted at method granularity and
// the call-edge layer labels it `runtime_observed`, which is what tells a consumer
// that it has no written call site behind it.
caller_has_blind_spot(m) :- method_has_unknown_call(m, _).

runtime_closes_blind_spot(callerM, calleeM) :-
    runtime_edge_method(callerM, calleeM, _),
    caller_has_blind_spot(callerM),
    !static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM).

// The static answer, at method granularity, so "the engine already named it" is a
// question that can be asked.
static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM) :-
    client_calls_client("client", e, "client", calleeM),
    call_from("client", e, callerM).
static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM) :-
    accessor_edge("client", e, calleeM, _),
    call_from_expr("client", e, callerM).

// An observed edge the engine did not name AND whose caller has no unresolved site
// either. This is the more interesting of the two: the engine believed it had
// resolved everything in that method and was wrong, or the call came from a path
// with no syntax at all (a compiler-generated state machine, an interface
// dispatched by a container). Kept separate so the two causes are not summed.
runtime_unexplained_edge(callerM, calleeM) :-
    runtime_edge_method(callerM, calleeM, _),
    !caller_has_blind_spot(callerM),
    !static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM).

// ── (2) A FAN THE RUN NARROWED ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// The engine named several targets from one caller under one simple name and the
// run only ever took one. THE FAN IS NOT CHANGED. This records which member was
// seen, so a consumer ranking chains can prefer it, and so the count of such sites
// is a measurable statement about how much precision a runtime signal could buy.
static_fan_member(callerM, n, calleeM) :-
    static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM),
    method_name("client", calleeM, n).
static_fan_width(callerM, n, c) :-
    static_fan_member(callerM, n, _),
    c = count : { static_fan_member(callerM, n, _) }.
runtime_fan_taken(callerM, n, calleeM) :-
    static_fan_member(callerM, n, calleeM),
    runtime_edge_method(callerM, calleeM, _).
runtime_fan_taken_count(callerM, n, c) :-
    runtime_fan_taken(callerM, n, _),
    c = count : { runtime_fan_taken(callerM, n, _) }.

// The fan is wide and exactly one member was ever taken.
runtime_narrowable_fan(callerM, n, calleeM) :-
    static_fan_width(callerM, n, w), w >= 2,
    runtime_fan_taken_count(callerM, n, 1),
    runtime_fan_taken(callerM, n, calleeM).

// ── (3) A TYPE THE RUN SHOWS IS CONSTRUCTED ─────────────────────────────────
// type_instantiated is derived statically from `new` sites, and a type built by
// reflection or by a container has none. Observing its constructor run is direct
// evidence it exists at runtime.
//
// This ADDS to type_instantiated, which is exported and NOT used to filter the
// dispatch fan -- for the reason stated in virtual-dispatch.dl: narrowing by
// observed instantiation is unsound on any dependency-injected application, and a
// trace makes that no safer, only more tempting.
type_instantiated("client", gk) :-
    runtime_edge_method(_, ctor, _),
    method_is_ctor("client", ctor),
    method_in_type("client", gk, ctor).

// ── WHAT THE RUN CONFIRMED ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A static edge the run took. Exported because it is the strongest evidence a
// static edge is real, and because the ratio of confirmed to named is the honest
// way to describe how much of a graph a workload actually exercises.
runtime_confirms_edge(callerM, calleeM) :-
    runtime_edge_method(callerM, calleeM, _),
    static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM).

// A static edge the run did NOT take. NOT A DEFECT AND NOT EXPORTED AS ONE: the
// workload simply did not go there. Named here so that nothing downstream is
// tempted to treat the absence as a finding.
// A one-column marker rather than a nullary relation: souffle declares relations
// with at least one attribute, and the generator infers the arity from the head, so
// a 0-arity head is a mismatch rather than a fact.
runtime_trace_present("yes") :- runtime_observed_edge(_, _, _).
runtime_did_not_execute(callerM, calleeM) :-
    static_edge_exists(callerM, calleeM),
    runtime_trace_present("yes"),
    !runtime_edge_method(callerM, calleeM, _).
